1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,537
Welcome to practicals twenty nine and thirty:

2
00:00:03,537 --> 00:00:07,546
the full pipeline and an industry-style submission.

3
00:00:07,546 --> 00:00:10,848
This first video builds the full pipeline.

4
00:00:11,648 --> 00:00:15,293
Practical twenty nine builds the full pipeline:

5
00:00:15,293 --> 00:00:22,196
audit, hypotheses, split, cross-validation, a leakage demo, skew, and the final pipeline.

6
00:00:22,196 --> 00:00:29,021
Practical thirty is an industry-style submission with a worked example on diabetes data.

7
00:00:29,021 --> 00:00:42,128
The main file is Online Shoppers, twelve thousand three hundred thirty rows by eighteen columns; the submission example uses diabetes, seven hundred sixty eight by nine.

8
00:00:42,928 --> 00:00:43,622
Step one:

9
00:00:43,622 --> 00:00:44,779
load and audit.

10
00:00:44,779 --> 00:00:53,030
Twelve thousand three hundred thirty rows by eighteen columns, with one hundred twenty five duplicate rows.

11
00:00:53,030 --> 00:01:00,664
Drop them and we have twelve thousand two hundred five rows, with fifteen point six percent buyers.

12
00:01:01,464 --> 00:01:02,181
Step two:

13
00:01:02,181 --> 00:01:04,410
test the refined hypotheses.

14
00:01:04,410 --> 00:01:16,196
Group by has page value gives buy rates of point oh three nine and point five six three; a chi-square test on the cross-tab gives a p-value of zero.

15
00:01:16,196 --> 00:01:29,495
Group by visitor type gives new point two four nine, other point one nine eight, returning point one four one, with p of about four times ten to the minus twenty nine.

16
00:01:29,495 --> 00:01:34,512
Both p-values are below point oh five, so keep H one and H two.

17
00:01:35,312 --> 00:01:36,119
Step three:

18
00:01:36,119 --> 00:01:37,954
build features and split.

19
00:01:37,954 --> 00:01:45,881
One-hot encode month and visitor type, giving twelve thousand two hundred five rows by twenty eight columns.

20
00:01:45,881 --> 00:01:59,240
A stratified eighty twenty split gives nine thousand seven hundred sixty four training rows and two thousand four hundred forty one test rows, with point one five six buyers in each.

21
00:02:00,040 --> 00:02:00,787
Step four:

22
00:02:00,787 --> 00:02:08,932
a pipeline of Standard Scaler and logistic regression, scored with five-fold cross-validation on R O C A U C.

23
00:02:08,932 --> 00:02:24,774
The five scores are point eight nine three, point nine oh two, point eight eight, point eight eight seven and point eight eight nine, with a mean of point eight nine and a standard deviation of point oh oh seven.

24
00:02:24,774 --> 00:02:30,304
Only training rows are used, and the scaler is re-fitted inside each fold.

25
00:02:31,104 --> 00:02:31,933
Step five:

26
00:02:31,933 --> 00:02:33,343
the leakage demo.

27
00:02:33,343 --> 00:02:39,811
Over-sample the buyers before the split, then split and train a random forest.

28
00:02:39,811 --> 00:02:43,128
The leaky recall is point nine nine two.

29
00:02:43,128 --> 00:02:52,416
But checking the test buyers, ninety eight point four percent are copies of training rows, so the score is fake.

30
00:02:53,216 --> 00:02:54,025
Step six:

31
00:02:54,025 --> 00:02:55,373
the honest way.

32
00:02:55,373 --> 00:03:03,374
Split first, then over-sample only the training rows, and test on the untouched test set.

33
00:03:03,374 --> 00:03:10,925
Honest recall is point six six five, versus point nine nine two for the leaky order.

34
00:03:10,925 --> 00:03:13,352
Split first, then resample.

35
00:03:14,152 --> 00:03:15,060
Step seven:

36
00:03:15,060 --> 00:03:15,472
skew.

37
00:03:15,472 --> 00:03:21,249
An always-no model has accuracy point eight four four and recall zero.

38
00:03:21,249 --> 00:03:32,224
A random two percent sample has eighteen percent buyers, while a stratified two percent sample has fifteen point six, the true share.

39
00:03:33,024 --> 00:03:33,970
Step eight:

40
00:03:33,970 --> 00:03:36,551
three ways to handle the skew.

41
00:03:36,551 --> 00:03:41,025
A score function reports accuracy, recall and F one.

42
00:03:41,025 --> 00:03:44,638
No change gives recall point four one six.

43
00:03:44,638 --> 00:03:49,026
Class weight balanced gives point seven eight five.

44
00:03:49,026 --> 00:03:55,393
Under-sampling point seven eight five, over-sampling point seven nine one.

45
00:03:55,393 --> 00:04:04,512
Accuracy drops slightly, from point eight eight nine to about point eight five, but recall nearly doubles.

46
00:04:05,312 --> 00:04:06,128
Step nine:

47
00:04:06,128 --> 00:04:08,820
insight check and final pipeline.

48
00:04:08,820 --> 00:04:16,734
November, October and September have the highest buy rates, with November at point two five five.

49
00:04:16,734 --> 00:04:29,381
The final pipeline is a scaler and class-weighted logistic regression, with cross-validation R O C A U C of point nine zero plus or minus point oh oh five.

50
00:04:29,381 --> 00:04:32,644
We fit once and score the test set once:

51
00:04:32,644 --> 00:04:39,824
accuracy point eight five one, recall point seven eight five, F one point six two three.

52
00:04:40,624 --> 00:04:41,326
Step ten:

53
00:04:41,326 --> 00:04:43,509
what drives the final model?

54
00:04:43,509 --> 00:04:56,062
The top weights are page values at two point three, month November at point three nine, and product related duration and administrative pages with small weights.

55
00:04:56,062 --> 00:05:07,680
The E D A insight, valued pages fifty six percent versus four percent, and the model's top feature agree, and so do the November peak and its weight.

56
00:05:07,680 --> 00:05:13,528
E D A and the model tell the same story, which makes a trustworthy insight.

